A", "role": "Headquarters"}, {"name": "Los Angeles, USA", "role": "Cultural Hub"}, {"name": "Tokyo, Japan", "role": "Origin of Content"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Viz Media", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "viz-media"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q660288", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viz_Media"], "tags": ["United States", "publishing house", "Americas", "Media & Publishing Houses", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Viz Media contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Viz Media significantly shaped the regional landscape of entertainment by establishing a thriving market for manga and anime in North America, influencing both consumer tastes and industry standards.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-25T16:52:00Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30166403594", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "American entertainment company Headquartered in San Francisco. Founded 1986.", "newValue": "Viz Media, founded in 1986 and headquartered in San Francisco, is a pivotal American entertainment company that specializes in the publication of manga, anime, and related merchandise. As a subsidiary of the larger Viz Group, it plays a crucial role in the North American cultural landscape, particularly in the dissemination of Japanese pop culture. Key figures in its establishment include Seiji Horibuchi, who recognized the potential of anime and manga markets beyond Asia. Through strategic partnerships and acquisitions, Viz Media has become a leading distributor of Japanese media, significantly influencing the entertainment industry in North America.

The company's inception coincided with the growing popularity of anime and manga in the West, catalyzed by landmark titles such as 'Dragon Ball' and 'Naruto.' By the late 1990s and early 2000s, Viz Media had secured licensing agreements with major Japanese publishers and studios, allowing it to publish English-language versions of popular series and films. This not only broadened the audience for these works but also set standards for localization and marketing in the industry. In 2002, Viz Media launched the 'Shonen Jump' magazine, which became a cultural phenomenon, further solidifying its place in American pop culture.

